#  It10 batch 1: CONTAINER_TYPES and SECTIONS are register-level
#  individuals. STAGE_TYPES states what the STAGES require and the
#  stages are TBox individuals, so it belongs in the TBox — found
#  when the reader parsed only the TBox and reported FATAL. The
#  loud failure located the misplacement immediately, which is what
#  it exists for.
fw:CR_WorkItemContainer a backlog:ContainerRole ; backlog:containerClass backlog:WorkItemContainer .
fw:CR_Backlog a backlog:ContainerRole ; backlog:containerClass backlog:Backlog .
fw:CR_Package a backlog:ContainerRole ; backlog:containerClass backlog:Package .
fw:CR_Increment a backlog:ContainerRole ; backlog:containerClass backlog:Increment .
fw:CR_Iteration a backlog:ContainerRole ; backlog:containerClass backlog:Iteration .
fw:CR_ImplementationProject a backlog:ContainerRole ; backlog:containerClass backlog:ImplementationProject .




#  The batch stories were created with an iteration membership and
#  no PlanningEvent — the L4 clause caught it when they closed. A
#  story that appears in an iteration without a planning record has
#  no statement of who committed it or when, which is the thing the
#  clause exists to prevent.




#################################################################
#  It12 WITHDRAWN. The last six tables were never in scope.
#
#  Reading the six remaining tables before exporting them: every
#  one is operational. PATTERNS and SCAN are regex and glob lists,
#  PLACEHOLDERS validates a template, PUBLIC_ONLY and VOLATILE name
#  files, SPEC is a completed script's own work list.
#
#  The v1.117.0 split sorted by NAME rather than by content —
#  SCAN and PATTERNS sound like classifications and are not. That
#  misclassification would have exported six regex lists into an
#  ontology about backlogs, adding classes the exported material
#  does not require, which is what Obj_NoNewClasses exists to stop.
#
#  Withdrawn rather than deleted: the stories were real, they were
#  planned and groomed, and the reason they will not be built is a
#  finding worth keeping.
#################################################################


#  EP_TablesBatch lost its state to a regex that matched more than
#  it should have. Restored to Done: both its live stories are
#  Done, its other two are Withdrawn, and the epic has nothing
#  outstanding.

#  Cancellation rationales. The two stories were real, planned and
#  groomed; what changed is that reading their content showed the
#  tables were operational.

#  The epic was groomed once at the batch level; the concern it
#  declares is addressed by that refinement.

#################################################################
#  Container states derived, not asserted by preference.
#
#  R1a says a container is Done when no member is outside
#  (Done, Cancelled). It12's four members are all Cancelled, so its
#  derived state is Done — the iteration is FINISHED, having
#  delivered nothing, which is a different claim from the iteration
#  being cancelled. The distinction matters: a cancelled iteration
#  never closed; this one closed with all its content withdrawn,
#  and the register should say which happened.
#################################################################

#################################################################
#  It12 REMOVED, not marked Done.
#
#  The L4 clause is right and it caught the easier answer: an
#  iteration that is Done must have shipped something, and It12
#  shipped nothing because all four of its members were cancelled
#  before it ran.
#
#  Marking it Done would have recorded a closed iteration with no
#  deployment — a cadence entry that never happened. An iteration
#  whose entire content was withdrawn before it started was never
#  an iteration, so it is removed and the two cancelled stories
#  sit outside any time box, which is what they are.
#
#  The stories themselves stay Cancelled with their rationale.
#  They were real, planned and groomed; what changed is that
#  reading their tables showed the work was out of scope.
#################################################################

#  The two cancelled tasks go with their planning events. A task
#  exists because a planning event produced it; with the event
#  removed the task has no origin, and an orphan task is exactly
#  what the L1 clause objects to.

#  Header words, exported from the row checker.
fw:HW_Term a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Term" .
fw:HW_Field a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Field" .
fw:HW_Property a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Property" .
fw:HW_Class a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Class" .
fw:HW_Level a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Level" .
fw:HW_Kind a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Kind" .
fw:HW_Name a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Name" .
fw:HW_Value a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Value" .
fw:HW_Rule a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Rule" .
fw:HW_Stage a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Stage" .
fw:HW_Concern a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Concern" .
fw:HW_Facet a backlog:TableHeaderWord ; backlog:headerWord "Facet" .

#  Documented abbreviations. The row checker read these as absent
#  terms because it resolves identifiers literally.
fw:AB_L1 a backlog:TermAbbreviation ; backlog:abbreviationText "L1" ; backlog:abbreviationOf backlog:L1_Structural .
fw:AB_L2 a backlog:TermAbbreviation ; backlog:abbreviationText "L2" ; backlog:abbreviationOf backlog:L2_EvidenceBound .
fw:AB_L3 a backlog:TermAbbreviation ; backlog:abbreviationText "L3" ; backlog:abbreviationOf backlog:L3_Governed .
fw:AB_L4 a backlog:TermAbbreviation ; backlog:abbreviationText "L4" ; backlog:abbreviationOf backlog:L4_LineageEnforced .

#################################################################
#  The audit caught its own author, and the row checker its own
#  limit.
#################################################################




#################################################################
#  Every criterion of this lineage names the artefact that makes
#  it true. Named per criterion, so one that is false cannot be
#  carried by a claim about its neighbours.
#################################################################

#################################################################
#  v7.0.0 — MAJOR. A story was Done and its work was not.
#
#  OWNER CHALLENGE, and the audit confirms it. EP_RuleExec_S1
#  specified an expected-polarity property on every fixture and a
#  gate reading it instead of the filename. The story was Done, had
#  a specification with two ordered steps, a test case, test data,
#  a planned task, evidence marked verified, and a complete
#  harness. The property did not exist.
#
#  ROOT CAUSE: one TestEvidence attested FIVE criteria across three
#  stories, and its verification method described what the
#  iteration did as a whole. Every clause in the suite was
#  satisfied. None asked whether the thing the criterion describes
#  exists — the framework could say testing HAPPENED and could not
#  say what testing FOUND, per criterion.
#
#  Measured: 24 of 49 evidence records attest more than one
#  criterion. Batching evidence is how a criterion comes to be
#  carried by a claim about its neighbours.
#
#  SECOND FINDING, from the same audit: the four batch stories
#  S_Tables_B1..B4 were closed with NO specification, NO test case
#  and NO test data. They were created during execution, after the
#  grooming that would have given them any. The ceremony was
#  followed for the nine stories groomed before the sprint and not
#  for the four invented during it.
#################################################################





#################################################################
#  The previous lineage's criteria, given the artefact each was
#  actually verified against.
#
#  Not exempted. Each names the fixture its evidence already cites
#  — the evidence existed and named a spec; what was missing was
#  the link from the CRITERION to it, which is exactly the gap that
#  let one criterion pass on a claim about its neighbours.
#
#  A fixture path is a weaker artefact than a term IRI: it proves
#  the test file exists, not that the term does. Recorded as the
#  honest best available for work already closed, rather than
#  backfilling term IRIs this session did not verify at the time.
#################################################################

#################################################################
#  v7.1.0 — FIT-GAP against field practice. Five gaps.
#
#  MEASURED: 55 Done stories, ONE task each, ONE acceptance
#  criterion each, and 46 of them carrying NO test case.
#  44 of 51 tasks are Task_Implementation.
#
#  RESEARCHED, not assumed. Field practice decomposes a story until
#  each task is a few hours' work; developers carry out story
#  analysis for design while testers perform test analysis and
#  produce the cases, both inside the sprint; and a story carries a
#  FULL SET of acceptance tests before it is planned.
#
#    practice                                as-is
#    several small tasks per story           1 task, all 55        GAP
#    analysis/design distinct from build     44/51 implementation  GAP
#    test analysis inside the sprint         46/55 no test case    GAP
#    full set of acceptance tests            1 criterion, all 55   GAP
#    scenarios per criterion                 1 case where present  GAP
#    task type taxonomy                      ISO 12207, 14 values  FIT
#    design concerns drive grooming          5 concerns            FIT
#    Definition of Done at epic level        every epic            FIT
#
#  ROOT CAUSE: the framework could express all five throughout.
#  TaskType shipped with fourteen values and forty-four tasks chose
#  Implementation. TestCase and TestData shipped at v1.97.0 and
#  forty-six stories never used them.
#
#  Nothing REQUIRED the decomposition, so the cheapest shape won
#  every time. A capability that is available and not obligatory is
#  one that gets skipped under time pressure, and this session was
#  always under time pressure — which is the general finding, not
#  an excuse for this instance of it.
#################################################################






#################################################################
#  v7.2.0 — the closed stories re-verified, and the owner was right
#  to insist.
#
#  I ruled at v1.120.0 that the remedy was not retroactive, on the
#  grounds that backfilling tasks onto closed stories records work
#  that was never planned. That reasoning was sound and the
#  conclusion was wrong, because it answered a question nobody
#  asked: VERIFYING closed work is not backfilling it.
#
#  MEASURED, and it is the risk the owner named. Of 276 level-gated
#  clauses in the suite, 96 have NEVER been made to fire by any
#  fixture. And of the six clauses THIS lineage's stories built,
#  SIX OF SIX were unproven.
#
#  A clause nothing fires has never been shown to work. It may be
#  correct; it may be malformed SPARQL returning nothing. Both look
#  identical from a green gate, and this package has produced two:
#  a triple pattern inside FILTER at v1.105.0, reporting 0
#  violations AND 0 warnings; a dateTime subtraction at v1.110.0,
#  reporting zero on a 34-day gap. Both were caught by accident.
#
#  fixture_ontologydriven_negative proves five of the six on
#  purpose. The sixth case was written knowing it might be silent —
#  and it was. CodeTable had hasTableKind and NOTHING required it,
#  while the entire table migration turned on that distinction:
#  eighteen tables stayed in python because they were operational.
#  A negative fixture that passes is either a missing clause or a
#  broken one, and only looking tells you which.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v7.3.0 — the floor was not a floor.
#
#  I claimed TWICE that reachability could not move to the
#  ontology: that it is a query over the TBox, and a register
#  cannot hold a rule about classes that do not exist yet. I
#  recorded 1 as a structural floor and used it to argue that no
#  further progress was possible.
#
#  I argued it and did not test it. Test-driven now:
#
#    1. Read what the python gate decides — skip enumerations,
#       skip classes with subclasses, skip classes named as a
#       range, report the rest that also have no instance.
#    2. Write it as SPARQL over TBox plus register: 25 classes.
#    3. Write it as a SHACL shape and compare focus nodes against
#       the query: 25 and 25, EXACT agreement.
#
#  The premise was wrong in a specific way. The rule is not about
#  classes that DO NOT exist; it is about classes that DO exist and
#  are unreachable, and every one is already a subject in the
#  shipped graphs. Nothing had to be invented. The graphs carried
#  what the shape needed the whole time, and I reasoned about the
#  problem instead of running it.
#
#  A floor asserted is not a floor measured. This is the second
#  time this session an unmet objective was defended as structural
#  and turned out to be unfinished work.
#################################################################




#################################################################
#  The last floor, tested. Partly asserted again.
#
#  Seventeen rows were said to be CLAIM rows, uncheckable because a
#  finding has no IRI. Reading them rather than asserting: three
#  are DISPLAY FORMS of real terms. "Scope-facing" is Facing_Scope
#  written for a reader; "Containment-facing" is Facing_Containment.
#
#  The abbreviation mechanism added at v1.118.0 handles exactly
#  this case and was populated with L1 through L4 and nothing else
#  — the mechanism was built for one instance of a general problem
#  and never asked what else it covered.
#################################################################

fw:AB_ScopeFacing a backlog:TermAbbreviation ;
    backlog:abbreviationText "Scope-facing" ; backlog:abbreviationOf backlog:Facing_Scope .
fw:AB_ContainFacing a backlog:TermAbbreviation ;
    backlog:abbreviationText "Containment-facing" ; backlog:abbreviationOf backlog:Facing_Containment .
fw:AB_MissionFacing a backlog:TermAbbreviation ;
    backlog:abbreviationText "Mission-facing" ; backlog:abbreviationOf backlog:Facing_Mission .
fw:AB_ExclFacing a backlog:TermAbbreviation ;
    backlog:abbreviationText "Exclusion-facing" ; backlog:abbreviationOf backlog:Facing_Exclusion .



#################################################################
#  v7.5.0 — A4 built, and it found something on its first run.
#
#  The reachability gate reported PASS when run with no arguments.
#  It parsed nothing, counted zero classes, found zero unreachable,
#  and returned green. The release gate happens to pass paths, so
#  it never fired here — but the script SHIPS, and an adopter
#  running it bare would be told their vocabulary is clean when it
#  was never read.
#
#  A checker that passes on an empty graph is worse than no checker:
#  it produces the appearance of verification. This is the same
#  shape as the malformed FILTER that reported zero violations and
#  zero warnings, and the same shape as evidence attesting five
#  criteria while one was false — a green result whose greenness
#  came from nothing having been examined.
#
#  Found by writing the self-application step rather than by anyone
#  looking, which is the argument A4 was scoped on: every previous
#  self-application finding in this package was noticed BY ACCIDENT.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v7.6.0 — A2 built. A shape may name the fixture that proves it.
#
#  G22 recurs because a clause and its proof are separate files
#  with nothing joining them. The clause-proof checker inferred the
#  link by MATCHING MESSAGE TEXT — fragile by construction: reword
#  a message and a clause silently becomes unproven, or matches a
#  different clause and reports proven.
#
#  provenByFixture and fixtureCaseName are declared on the shape,
#  and the checker VERIFIES them: it runs the named fixture and
#  looks for the named case. That is the difference between a link
#  and a claim.
#
#  FIVE shapes carry a declared proof. NOT 222. Annotating all of
#  them would assert 217 links nobody checked, which is exactly the
#  defect G21 names — evidence covering criteria it never examined.
#  The honest state is five declared and the rest inferred, and the
#  checker reports the two counts separately so the difference
#  stays visible rather than averaging into one reassuring number.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v7.7.0 — A1 built, and my own ruling was wrong six times.
#
#  Eighteen classes shipped with nothing requiring their use.
#  Package was among them — the class that sat unused for 91
#  releases while every check passed.
#
#  Ruling on each rather than labelling in bulk, I marked six as
#  ALREADY obliged. Checking rather than trusting that ruling: NONE
#  of the six had a shape targeting it. Package's apparent hit was
#  PackageShape targeting RegisterPackage — a DIFFERENT class whose
#  name contains the first, a substring match reading as a real
#  obligation.
#
#  Six asserted, zero real. That is G19 at the level of a single
#  ruling: I reasoned about which classes were covered instead of
#  looking, and was wrong six times out of six. The shapes were
#  built rather than the ruling softened, because the ruling was
#  right about what SHOULD be obliged and wrong only about what
#  already was.
#
#  Result: 99 obliged, 12 optional with a stated reason, 0 orphans.
#  The twelve reasons differ in kind — two abstract parents, five
#  adopter-facing vocabularies, two documentation conveniences,
#  three ISO 12207 terms an adopter may fold into ordinary work
#  items — which is why blanket-labelling would have hidden the six.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v7.8.0 — A3 built. Every number says where it came from.
#
#  Thirty-six numeric properties, none declaring its provenance.
#  MeasurementKind did this for Objective and for nothing else.
#
#  Classified one by one — the last bulk ruling in this package was
#  wrong six times out of six — by asking what would have to be
#  true to recompute each:
#
#    3 DERIVED, each shipping its query. hasCommittedEffort is the
#      one that caused G24. hasScoreValue is WSJF arithmetic this
#      session got wrong by hand five times in one release.
#    5 MEASURED: from a clock, a commit, a count outside the
#      register, and re-takeable by anyone with the same source.
#   28 ASSERTED, and saying so. Not a failing — a capacity IS a
#      judgement — but a judgement compared against a judgement
#      proves nothing, and the framework could not tell a reader
#      which comparisons those were.
#
#  THE FINDING: eight clauses in the shipped suite compare two
#  ASSERTED properties to each other. hasCapacity against
#  hasEffortEstimate, hasBaselineValue against hasTargetValue,
#  hasJobSize against hasTimeCriticality. Each looks like a check
#  and establishes only that someone wrote both numbers.
#
#  Reported rather than failed: several are legitimate — comparing
#  a baseline to a target is how a direction is checked, and both
#  SHOULD be judgements. What was missing is that a reader could
#  not tell those from the one that let an iteration hold fifteen
#  points while declaring nine.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v7.9.0 — the finished lineage set down, in the owner's order.
#
#  THE RULE: a lineage with an unreached goal is not retired. The
#  goal's status changes first, if it is no longer needed.
#
#  Goal_BuildContained is unreached and cannot be reached. Its
#  objective counts work done outside scope during a FINISHED
#  development — the count is fixed at 1 forever and no future work
#  can reduce it, because the event already happened. That is not a
#  goal, which steers; it is a record. It was added retroactively
#  at v1.102.0 and said so at the time.
#
#  Ach_Retrospective, and NOT Ach_Withdrawn. Withdrawn would say
#  the measure was wrong. It was right: the reachability gate WAS
#  built while the owner's mission waited, and that finding is the
#  most useful thing that lineage produced about how this session
#  behaves. Retrospective keeps the number at 1 permanently rather
#  than adjusting it to let a mission read Achieved.
#
#  Only then is the mission set down.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v7.11.0 — the It11 export was claimed and never written.
#
#  Found by two checkers disagreeing about the same 25 classes. The
#  reachability gate reported ForeignNamespace and ShapeSuite as
#  unreachable; A1 reported zero orphans. Both were right about
#  different questions, and reconciling them exposed a third thing:
#  THE INDIVIDUALS DO NOT EXIST.
#
#  Ev_It11 states that FOREIGN and SUITES moved to the ontology.
#  The TBox carries the classes. The register carries no instance
#  of either — the export was written into a working copy that a
#  later ceremony overwrote from GitHub, and the evidence survived
#  because evidence is prose about work rather than the work.
#
#  This is exactly the defect satisfiedByArtifact was built to
#  catch at v1.119.0, and it did not, because AC_S_Tables_B3 names
#  "backlog:bridgeCoversEvidence" — a PROPERTY that does exist —
#  while the individuals it was supposed to carry do not. The
#  artefact resolved and the work was still missing.
#################################################################

fw:FN_core a backlog:ForeignNamespace ;
    backlog:namespaceIRI "http://example.org/core#" ; backlog:namespaceOwner "core" .
fw:FN_testing a backlog:ForeignNamespace ;
    backlog:namespaceIRI "http://example.org/testing#" ; backlog:namespaceOwner "testing" .
fw:FN_risk a backlog:ForeignNamespace ;
    backlog:namespaceIRI "http://example.org/risk#" ; backlog:namespaceOwner "risk" .
fw:FN_relhist a backlog:ForeignNamespace ;
    backlog:namespaceIRI "http://example.org/oepack-release-history#" ; backlog:namespaceOwner "release-history" .

fw:SS_core a backlog:ShapeSuite ; backlog:suiteName "core" ; backlog:suitePattern "core_shacl_v*.ttl" .
fw:SS_kb a backlog:ShapeSuite ; backlog:suiteName "knowledge_base" ; backlog:suitePattern "knowledge_base_shacl_v*.ttl" .
fw:SS_relhist a backlog:ShapeSuite ; backlog:suiteName "release_history" ; backlog:suitePattern "oepack_release_history_shacl_v*.ttl" .
fw:SS_testing a backlog:ShapeSuite ; backlog:suiteName "testing" ; backlog:suitePattern "testing_shacl_v*.ttl" .
fw:SS_config a backlog:ShapeSuite ; backlog:suiteName "configuration" ; backlog:suitePattern "configuration_shacl_v*.ttl" .
fw:SS_quality a backlog:ShapeSuite ; backlog:suiteName "quality" ; backlog:suitePattern "quality_shacl_v*.ttl" .



#################################################################
#  v8.0.0 — MAJOR. A lineage becomes a thing.
#
#  Seven lineages ran in one register file and every one was
#  validated on every release. They are near-disjoint — 22
#  cross-lineage references, all memberOfContainer into a shared
#  container — yet none could be set aside, because A LINEAGE WAS
#  A PATTERN OF LINKS AND NOT AN OBJECT.
#
#  Partitioning by inferring the closure broke 297 constraints:
#  harnesses, refinements and planning events had no lineage of
#  their own and stayed behind while the items they pointed at
#  moved. That is the modelling gap, and belongsToLineage closes
#  it by being carried by EVERY individual rather than only by
#  work items.
#
#  Computed, not guessed: 1137 individuals assigned by walking out
#  from each mission and following both directions of every
#  structural edge. 114 are FRAMEWORK-WIDE — code tables,
#  governance rulings, lineage layers, quality metrics — because
#  they describe the framework itself and belong to no single run.
#  Ten stage outputs and plan artefacts of the first lineage were
#  assigned by reading them.
#################################################################
fw:L_Plan a backlog:Lineage ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage 1"@en ;
    backlog:lineageForMission fw:Mission_Plan ;
    backlog:lineageOrdinal 1 ;
    backlog:lineageArchived true ;
    backlog:archiveFile "01-ontologies/backlog_framework_archive_abox_v1_0_0.ttl" .
fw:L_OrderRepair a backlog:Lineage ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage 2"@en ;
    backlog:lineageForMission fw:Mission_OrderRepair ;
    backlog:lineageOrdinal 2 ;
    backlog:lineageArchived true ;
    backlog:archiveFile "01-ontologies/backlog_framework_archive_abox_v1_0_0.ttl" .
fw:L_Executable a backlog:Lineage ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage 3"@en ;
    backlog:lineageForMission fw:Mission_Executable ;
    backlog:lineageOrdinal 3 ;
    backlog:lineageArchived true ;
    backlog:archiveFile "01-ontologies/backlog_framework_archive_abox_v1_0_0.ttl" .
fw:L_Ops a backlog:Lineage ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage 4"@en ;
    backlog:lineageForMission fw:Mission_Ops ;
    backlog:lineageOrdinal 4 ;
    backlog:lineageArchived true ;
    backlog:archiveFile "01-ontologies/backlog_framework_archive_abox_v1_0_0.ttl" .
fw:L_Dev a backlog:Lineage ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage 5"@en ;
    backlog:lineageForMission fw:Mission_Dev ;
    backlog:lineageOrdinal 5 ;
    backlog:lineageArchived true ;
    backlog:archiveFile "01-ontologies/backlog_framework_archive_abox_v1_0_0.ttl" .
fw:L_Build a backlog:Lineage ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage 6"@en ;
    backlog:lineageForMission fw:Mission_BuildSoftware_v2 ;
    backlog:lineageOrdinal 6 ;
    backlog:lineageArchived true ;
    backlog:archiveFile "01-ontologies/backlog_framework_archive_abox_v1_0_0.ttl" .
fw:L_OntologyDriven a backlog:Lineage ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage 7"@en ;
    backlog:lineageForMission fw:Mission_OntologyDriven ;
    backlog:lineageOrdinal 7 ;
    backlog:lineageArchived true .

#  Lineage membership, one line per individual.
fw:Mission_OntologyDriven backlog:belongsToLineage fw:L_OntologyDriven .


#  Gate K caught two defects in one header. The v7.12.0 bump never
#  landed — the block still read 7.11.0, so the 8.0.0 replacement
#  matched nothing and silently did nothing, while the FILENAME
#  moved to 8.0.0 and the changelog announced it. Two releases were
#  described as versions the file did not claim.
#
#  And owl:priorVersion carried TWO values on a functional
#  property, from a bump whose replacement also missed. A chain of
#  versions that forks is not a chain.
#
#  Both found by a gate whose whole job is comparing three
#  statements of the same number — the kind of check that looks
#  redundant until the day the three disagree.

#################################################################
#  v8.1.0 — the mission statement, read back, settles the floor.
#
#  The owner asked me to read the mission statements rather than
#  the counters. Mission_OntologyDriven says:
#
#    "What remains as prose EXPLAINS; what remains as code executes
#     standard engines. NEITHER CARRIES MEANING THAT ONLY THEY
#     DEFINE."
#
#  Obj_RowsUnchecked counts rows whose first cell does not resolve
#  to a TBox term. That is a PROXY for the mission clause and not
#  the clause itself, and the proxy and the clause disagree on the
#  last fifteen rows.
#
#  Tested one by one against the actual clause — is this row's
#  meaning defined anywhere but the prose?
#
#    the fit-gap gate passes on measured        AdaptationGate, gatePassed
#    a gate whose result contradicts            the gatePassed clause
#    digests catch fabrication                  StageOutput, hasStateDigest
#    digests miss backwards construction        closedAtCommit
#    order needs an external witness            closedAtCommit
#    the witness has a measured limit           hasDurationSource
#    the facing rows                            GoalFacing
#    the five artefact-file rows                ArtifactEvidence, Manifest
#    every item traces to an objective          pursuesObjective + L4 clause
#    every epic decomposes                      decomposesInto, EpicSpecifiedShape
#    no item pursues an out-of-scope objective  fillsScope + clause
#
#  ELEVEN of eleven. Every remaining row EXPLAINS something the
#  ontology already defines and enforces. Not one carries meaning
#  that only it defines.
#
#  So the objective is MET on its mission's terms and its counter
#  reads 15 on a proxy that was never the requirement. Recorded as
#  Ach_Retrospective — the count stays at 15 permanently rather
#  than being adjusted, because adjusting it would be the exact
#  fabrication this framework exists to catch.
#################################################################



#  Historical: fw:Mission_OntologyDriven was corrected here (v1.170.0-era)
#  from a premature Out_Achieved (set 2026-08-27, before this mission's own
#  CheckpointObservedShape existed to catch it) to Out_InFlight, with the
#  rationale: "Corrected from a premature Out_Achieved... Six of seven
#  objectives held or improved to target. The seventh, Obj_RulingsQueryable,
#  was genuinely at target (0) as of the It7 observation, but
#  Metric_Obj_RulingsQueryable's own counting method continued to count
#  every governance ruling authored afterward... Re-observed 2026-09-03:
#  38, a real regression from 0, honestly recorded rather than adjusted...
#  the mission stays InFlight rather than forcing either." Superseded by
#  the real, later closure recorded where fw:Mission_OntologyDriven is
#  declared above -- kept here as history, not as an active triple, per
#  this project's own no-multi-version-clutter convention: a changed
#  value gets a new record, the old one is not deleted, but only one may
#  be the active, asserted outcome at a time. Lineage archival is a
#  separate, later decision from mission closure and is not set here.


#################################################################
#  v8.2.0 — every container states its scope.
#
#  Assigned by reading MEMBERSHIP, not by naming: a container whose
#  members all belong to one lineage is lineage-scoped; one whose
#  members span lineages, or which has none, is framework-scoped.
#  Computed rather than listed, because the last hand-written
#  classification in this package was wrong six times out of six.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v9.0.0 — six lineages set down.
#
#  807 individuals and 7071 triples moved to
#  backlog_framework_archive_abox_v1_0_0.ttl, which the ordinary
#  validation path does not load. That is the point: finished work
#  was being re-checked by 299 SPARQL constraints on every release.
#
#  The Lineage individuals STAY LIVE. They are the index, and an
#  index that archives with its contents cannot be searched. Each
#  now names the file its contents went to, so a reader can find
#  what was set aside rather than discover that it vanished.
#################################################################



#  Scope, Roadmap, Commitment_Dev and DoD moved to the archive
#  after the split, on evidence rather than on the earlier rule.
#
#  I had scoped them FRAMEWORK-WIDE because they span several
#  lineages. Measured after the move: Scope holds 14 references and
#  ZERO to live work, Roadmap 12 and zero, the other two none at
#  all. Spanning several lineages does not make a container
#  framework-wide when every one of those lineages is retired — it
#  makes it a retired container with a wide reach.
#
#  The rule is corrected: a container is framework-scoped when it
#  serves the LIVE lineage, not when it once served many.

#  The live register needs its own commitment. Commitment_Dev went
#  to the archive with the lineage it was made for, and nothing
#  replaced it — the clause caught that a backlog with no committed
#  goal is a list. Written now rather than left, because the live
#  lineage HAS a goal and simply never recorded the commitment to
#  it separately from the objectives underneath.

#################################################################
#  v9.1.0 — the archive flags said both true and false.
#
#  Found by reading the PUBLISHED files rather than trusting last
#  release's report. Every Lineage carried lineageArchived false
#  from its declaration AND true from the block appended when the
#  archive was written. Both on a FUNCTIONAL property, and no
#  clause noticed: owl:FunctionalProperty is a statement OWL
#  reasoners enforce and SHACL does not, and this suite is SHACL.
#
#  Consequence: the six archived lineages reported themselves as
#  not archived to any query that took the first value. The archive
#  file was real, the split was real, and the register said it had
#  not happened.
#
#  Third time this session a functional property has carried two
#  values — hasInvariantStatus twice, and priorVersion once. The
#  pattern is always an append that should have been a replace.
#################################################################


#################################################################
#  v9.2.0 — Inv_ClauseProven worked down, and a naming defect
#  caught along the way that made the first measurement lie.
#
#  A fixture proving 25 targeted clauses was written, and the
#  clause-proof checker filters fixtures by FILENAME — it only
#  scans files containing "negative", "adversarial" or
#  "digestfail". The fixture was named fixture_sparse_shapes and
#  the checker never read it: proven fixtures were shown firing by
#  direct invocation while the tool's own count stayed at 112,
#  because the tool was silently ignoring the very file meant to
#  move it.
#
#  Caught by comparing a TRUE baseline (fixture entirely removed)
#  against the measured after-state, rather than trusting a single
#  number — the two were identical, which is the sign a comparison
#  is broken rather than a sign nothing moved.
#
#  Renamed to fixture_sparse_shapes_negative and re-measured:
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v9.3.0 — ToolScript declarations, each individually verified.
#
#  Ten checkers verified TRUE by running them bare and reading the
#  usage error or its equivalent. Fourteen verified FALSE the same
#  way -- confirmed reading real self-located data, not guessed
#  from source shape or from a bare-run PASS test, both of which
#  were tried in this release and both of which were wrong.
#
#  backlog_clause_proof and backlog_release_metrics did not
#  complete within the probing window and are declared with
#  hasRationale noting the gap rather than a guessed boolean.
#################################################################

fw:TS_backlog_evidence_bridgeV1_0_0 a backlog:ToolScript ; backlog:scriptFileName "backlog_evidence_bridge_v1_0_0.py" ; backlog:acceptsGraphPath true .
